      "math_academy_id": "q-184080",
      "sequence_position": 6,
      "problem": "Suppose $n=6{m}^{5}+8{m}^{4}-1,$ where $m\\in Z.$ Complete the following argument.",
      "worked_solution": "An integer is *odd* if it can be written as $2a+1,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nNow, note the following regarding the expression $6{m}^{5}+8{m}^{4}-1:$\n\n- All terms, apart from the constant term, have a common factor of $2.$\n\n- The constant term can be written as $-1=-2+1.$\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is $\\text{odd}$ since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =6{m}^{5}+8{m}^{4}-1 \\\\  & =(6{m}^{5}+8{m}^{4}-2)+1 \\\\  & =2({{3{m}^{5}+4{m}^{4}-1}_{⏟}}_{a})+1 \\\\  & =2a+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a=3{m}^{5}+4{m}^{4}-1$ is an integer.",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
